      "text": "To find the value of ( t ), we need to determine how the roots change from one polynomial to another.\n\n1. **Calculate the coefficients related to ( t )**:\n   - The coefficient ( r ) in the second polynomial is calculated as ( r = -(a + b + c) ).\n   - The coefficient ( s ) in the second polynomial is calculated as ( s = ab + bc + ca ).\n\n2. **Identify the roots of the new polynomial**:\n   - The roots of the new polynomial are:\n     - ( a + b )\n     - ( b + c )\n     - ( c + a )\n\n3. **Calculate ( t ) using Vieta's formulas**:\n   - According to Vieta's formulas, the constant term ( t ) of the new polynomial can also be obtained by the product of the sums of the roots taken three at a time.\n   - For roots ( x_1, x_2, x_3 ), the value of ( t ) is given by ( x_1 cdot x_2 cdot x_3 ).\n   - Substituting the roots ( a + b, b + c, c + a ), we get:\n     [\n     t = (a + b) cdot (b + c) cdot (c + a)\n     ]\n\n4. **Return the value of ( t )**.\n\nSo, the value of ( t ) is obtained as ( (a + b) cdot (b + c) cdot (c + a) ).\n\nBy calculating the roots ( a + b, b + c, c + a ) and using the product of these roots, we get ( t = (a + b) cdot (b + c) cdot (c + a) ).\n\nTherefore, the answer to the problem is ( t = (a + b) cdot (b + c) cdot (c + a) ).",
