      "text": "From the given polynomial $x^3 + 3x^2 + 4x - 11 = 0$, and the fact that it represents the polynomial with roots $a,b,c$, we have the following formulas from Vieta's theorem:\n\n\\begin{align*}\na+b+c&=-3\\\\\nab+ac+bc&=4\\\\\nabc&=11.\n\\end{align*}\n\nFor the second polynomial, we can make the substitution $x' = x-a$, making the roots $x' = a+b, a+c,  b+c$. Expanding $(x-a)^3 (x-b)(x-c)$ yields\n\n\\begin{align*}\nx^3 - a(x^2 + bc) - b(x^2 + ac) - c(x^2 + ab) + (a-b)(b+c)(a+c) &=0\\\\\nx^3 + (a+b+c) x^2 + (ab+bc+ac)x + (-a^2b-b^2c-c^2a) &=0\n\\end{align*}\n\nComparing with the given equation $x^3 + rx^2 + sx +t = 0$, we have that $t = -abc = \\boxed{-11}$.\n\nThe answer is: -11",
